To regenerate every compatibility pickle answer file, from the repo root run:

    ./integration/compatibility/regenerate.sh

Or, to regenerate just one, cd to this directory and run pytest directly:

    pytest generate.py         # produces aggregate-answers.pickle.gz
    pytest generate_text.py    # produces text-search-answers.pickle.gz
    pytest generate_hybrid.py  # produces hybrid-answers.pickle.gz

All forms require Docker. Each generator spins up a reference engine on port
6380 to capture answers; the docker handling is sometimes flaky and may need to
be killed manually between runs.

The reference image is per-generator (BaseCompatibilityTest.DOCKER_IMAGE):
generate.py and generate_text.py use redis/redis-stack-server, while
generate_hybrid.py uses redis:8, because FT.HYBRID does not exist in the
redis-stack image's RediSearch 2.x.

Each pickle stores a SHA256 of every .py file in this directory. The
compatibility integration test (integration/compatibility_test.py) verifies
that hash on load and fails if it does not match the current sources -- this
forces a regeneration whenever generate.py, generate_text.py,
generate_hybrid.py, data_sets.py, text_query_builder.py, or any other source
here is edited. Because the hash covers the whole directory, editing any one of
them invalidates *all* the pickles, so regenerate.sh regenerates all of them.

To add a new generator: create generate_xxx.py (subclass BaseCompatibilityTest
with its own ANSWER_FILE_NAME, and DOCKER_IMAGE / CONTAINER_NAME if it needs a
different reference engine) and add an entry to the GENERATORS list in
__init__.py. regenerate.sh and compatibility_test.py both read from that list.

To bypass the hash check (e.g. when generating a small pickle locally for
quick iteration), set:

    SKIP_COMPATIBILITY_HASH_CHECK=1
